<%# locals: (playthrough:, scene:, feedback:) %>
<%# THE INSTRUMENT, as small as it can be and still be one click.

    A `<footer>` and NOT a `<div>`, which is load-bearing rather than semantic
    taste: the log's dimming rule is `.log:not(.streaming) > .turn:last-of-type`
    and `:last-of-type` counts elements of the same tag among their siblings, so
    a `<div>` here would become the last div in the log and the newest turn
    would stop reading at full strength. Three tests assert that selector
    directly.

    Its own Turbo Stream target, per turn, so recording a verdict replaces this
    element and nothing else -- the prose does not reflow, the input keeps
    focus, and a turn streaming into `#stream` below is untouched.

    TWO FORMS, and each has exactly one meaning. The verdict form is three
    submit buttons sharing the name `verdict`, so a click sends one value and
    nothing else: one click, no second step, and no state to get out of date.
    The note form appears only once there is a verdict to attach it to -- the
    order is the point, since a judgement that has to be written out before it
    counts is a judgement that will not get recorded mid-play. Enter in the note
    box submits it, because that form has one submit button and it is `save`.

    A note being typed while a turn lands is lost, because `NarrationJob`
    replaces the whole of `#turn_log` when it does. The window is small -- taking
    the next turn means leaving this box for the command input -- and closing it
    would mean either holding draft state in JavaScript or narrowing what the
    broadcast replaces, both of which cost more than they save. %>
<footer class="verdict" id="<%= dom_id(scene, :verdict) %>">
  <%= form_with url: playthrough_feedbacks_path(playthrough), class: "marks" do |form| %>
    <%= form.hidden_field :scene_id, value: scene.id, id: nil %>
    <% Playthrough::Feedback::VERDICTS.each do |verdict| %>
      <%# The label IS the value: `name: "verdict"` puts the button's own value
          on the wire, so three buttons in one form are three answers to one
          question. `chosen` is read from the record rather than remembered
          anywhere, which is what makes amending it work by re-rendering. %>
      <%= form.submit verdict, name: "verdict",
                               class: class_names("mark", verdict, chosen: feedback&.verdict == verdict) %>
    <% end %>
  <% end %>

  <% if feedback %>
    <%= form_with url: playthrough_feedbacks_path(playthrough), class: "why" do |form| %>
      <%= form.hidden_field :scene_id, value: scene.id, id: nil %>
      <%# The verdict is carried unchanged: saving a note is not a change of
          mind, and `.record` amends only what it is given. %>
      <%= form.hidden_field :verdict, value: feedback.verdict, id: nil %>
      <%= form.text_field :note, value: feedback.note, autocomplete: "off",
                                 id: nil, placeholder: "why? (optional)" %>
      <%= form.submit "save" %>
    <% end %>

    <%# Clearing is the other half of amendable. A mis-click on a long log is
        noise in the measurement, and the alternative to taking one back is
        recording a verdict he does not mean. %>
    <%= button_to "clear", playthrough_feedback_path(playthrough, scene),
                  method: :delete, class: "clear" %>
  <% end %>
</footer>
